**Proposition 155.2 (Exact TV inequalities).** For a probability vector
$p$ and a word with first zero $n$,

$$
\operatorname{TV}(p,(1-a_n)e_0+a_ne_n)
=\max\{0,1-a_n-p(0),a_n-p(n),1-p(0)-p(n)\}.
\tag{155.2}
$$

Consequently the cap is equivalent to all rows
$p_{b_j}(E_j)\ge d_j(x)$, including the pair row. At $1^6$ it is
$p_{1^6}(0)\ge1-e$.

**Proof.** TV is the largest target deficit over events. Removing labels
outside the two-point target support can only increase that deficit.
The four possible subsets of the support give exactly (155.2).
For the one-point target only its singleton can have a positive deficit.
There are $3\cdot63+1=190$ rows. A negative singleton demand is legal
and redundant, but it is retained in the indexed system. The pair demand
cannot be recovered merely by adding the two singleton demands, which
would lose another allowance $e$. $\square$

